TI  - Outcomes of nonagenarians undergoing hip fracture surgery: a multicentre 
      observational study across Asia-Oceana.
LID - 10.1186/s12877-026-07157-8 [doi]
LID - 413
AB  - BACKGROUND: Nonagenarians with hip fracture represent a rapidly growing, highly 
      vulnerable subgroup, yet comparative data across diverse health‑care systems in 
      Asia‑Oceania are scarce. We aimed to describe mortality, postoperative 
      complications, and perioperative care patterns in nonagenarians undergoing hip 
      fracture surgery in Australia and Malaysia, and to explore associations between 
      key perioperative factors and mortality. METHODS: We conducted a binational, 
      retrospective, multicentre observational study of consecutive nonagenarians 
      (aged ≥ 90 to < 100 years) who underwent surgery for unilateral femoral neck, 
      intertrochanteric, or subtrochanteric fractures at four tertiary training 
      hospitals (one in Australia, three in Malaysia) between January 2016 and January 
      2021. Patient demographics, comorbidities, frailty, surgical and anaesthetic 
      details, and postoperative complications were extracted from electronic records; 
      complications were graded using the Clavien–Dindo classification. Primary 
      outcomes were in‑hospital, 30‑day, and 1‑year mortality; secondary outcomes were 
      number and severity of postoperative complications, length of hospital stay, and 
      30‑day readmission. Statistical analyses focused on descriptive comparisons and 
      cohort-stratified exploratory analyses to explore associations between 
      perioperative factors and mortality. RESULTS: Among 510 patients, 394 (77.3%) 
      were treated in Australia and 116 (22.7%) in Malaysia; median age was 92.0 years 
      (interquartile range 91.0–94.0). Baseline profiles and perioperative care 
      differed substantially: Malaysian patients more often had hypertension, diabetes, 
      and chronic kidney disease, whereas Australian patients more frequently had 
      congestive heart failure and dementia, and underwent emergency surgery within 
      48 h. In‑hospital and 30‑day mortality were similar between Australia and 
      Malaysia (6.3% vs. 6.9% and 8.6% vs. 10.3%, respectively), but 1‑year mortality 
      was higher in Malaysia (36.0% vs. 14.7%; P < 0.001). Overall, 86.0% of patients 
      experienced at least one postoperative complication, with higher rates in 
      Australia than Malaysia (98.3% vs. 49.1%), while severe complications 
      (Clavien–Dindo grade ≥ III) were comparable (15.0% vs. 15.5%). Median length of 
      stay was similar (8 days in both cohorts), but 30‑day readmission was more 
      frequent in Malaysia (28.7% vs. 2.7%; P < 0.001). In cohort‑stratified models, 
      factors associated with mortality differed by country and time point; no single 
      perioperative factor was consistently associated with mortality across settings. 
      CONCLUSIONS: In this binational cohort of nonagenarians undergoing hip fracture 
      surgery, short‑term mortality was comparable across four tertiary hospitals in 
      Australia and Malaysia, whereas 1‑year mortality and early readmission were 
      substantially higher in Malaysia. These findings suggest that system‑level 
      factors, including delayed access to surgery and limited coordinated post‑acute 
      care, may be important determinants of longer‑term outcomes in this very old 
      population, and highlight the need for context‑specific strategies to optimise 
      hip fracture care in resource‑diverse settings.
