TI  - An Insight Into a Potential Antithrombin-Independent Mechanism of Inadequate 
      Heparin Response in Neonates and Infants Undergoing Pediatric Cardiac Surgery: An 
      Exploratory Study.
PG  - 2675-2680
LID - S1053-0770(26)00459-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1053/j.jvca.2026.05.058 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVES: Inadequate heparin response is frequently observed in neonates and 
      young infants. While low antithrombin level is considered to be the primary 
      cause, the existing literature suggests that it is not necessarily responsible. 
      However, granular coagulation factor profiling data are rather limited. DESIGN: 
      Prospective, single-center study. SETTING: Tertiary academic hospital in the 
      United States. PARTICIPANTS: Total of 30 neonates and infants who underwent 
      cardiac surgery with cardiopulmonary bypass between May 31, 2022, and February 
      22, 2023. INTERVENTIONS: None. MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS: Comparison between 
      the heparin response group and the inadequate heparin response group demonstrated 
      that antithrombin levels were comparable. However, levels of another thrombin 
      inhibitor, α(2)-macroglobulin, and coagulation factor adjunct protein S were 
      significantly lower in the inadequate heparin response group. CONCLUSIONS: Lower 
      α(2)-macroglobulin and protein S levels may be at least partly responsible for 
      inadequate heparin response in the study cohort.
